BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Gardens": "https://gardens.duke.edu/event/on-the-rocks-a-scree-crevice-garden-tour/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:gardens
X-SOURCE:Sarah P. Duke Gardens
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261020T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261020T113000
DTSTAMP:20260915T130805Z
CREATED:20260706T122404Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260915T130805Z
UID:17472-1792490400-1792495800@gardens.duke.edu
SUMMARY:On the Rocks: A Scree & Crevice Garden Tour
DESCRIPTION:At first glance\, a pile of rocks seems like the last place a plant would want to call home—yet the scree and crevice gardens at Duke Gardens are bursting with an array of tenacious\, beautiful and cleverly adapted plants. Join Branden Kowalyszyn\, horticulturist of the Gateway Gardens at Duke Gardens\, for a guided tour through these rugged landscapes\, where sharp drainage\, shifting gravel and thin soils support an extraordinary community of plants from around the world. Explore the artistry and science that goes into creating these gardens\, including how the angle of a tilted stone\, the chemistry of the gravel and the geometry of a crevice allow these plants to live here. Learn how a plant’s form\, texture and growth habit reveal its survival strategies. Whether you’re a seasoned gardener looking for new ideas or someone who has walked past a rock garden and wondered what’s going on\, you’ll get an insider’s look at the horticultural craft required to create these habitats and leave with an appreciation for plants’ ability to thrive even in the hardest of places. \nFee: $24; free for Duke Gardens members with discount code. \nParking: Parking fees apply. \nLocation: Meet at the Barnes Welcome Center. \nAccessibility: This will be a small group program that takes place outdoors\, traveling on a combination of paved and unpaved paths with some uneven surfaces and moderate inclines.
URL:https://gardens.duke.edu/event/on-the-rocks-a-scree-crevice-garden-tour/
LOCATION:Duke Gardens–Barnes Welcome Center\, 420 Anderson Street\, Durham\, NC\, 27705\, United States
CATEGORIES:Adult Programs
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://gardens.duke.edu/wp-content/uploads/HG_Bodine_scree-succulent-5310.jpg
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar 1 Ics": "https://calendar.unc.edu/event/after-911-us-national-security-and-foreign-policy"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_1_ics
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261010T052518Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54099599575392
DTSTART:20261020T103000Z
DTEND:20261021T040000Z
DESCRIPTION:Twenty-five years ago\, the September 11\, 2001\, attacks in Ne
 w York and Washington\, D.C. shocked the world. Dramatic changes in Americ
 an life and foreign policy ensued\, including new domestic security measur
 es and the launching of the War on Terror.\n\nOur expert panel will explai
 n how the attacks transformed the United States and the world. Peter Feave
 r is a political scientist who served in George W. Bush’s White House. L
 andreth Freeman worked for the CIA for 22 years\, specializing in counteri
 ntelligence. Chris Kojm spent decades working in US foreign policy and nat
 ional security and served as deputy director of the 9/11 Commission. Josep
 h Stieb is a historian of U.S. military and foreign policy. Historian Mich
 ael Morgan will moderate the discussion.\n\nStudents who attend in person 
 can earn CLE credit. No tickets necessary\; seating is first-come\, first-
 served. Reserved tickets are available for guests coming from outside Chap
 el Hill\; email publicdiscourse@unc.edu to reserve seats. We will serve pi
 zza after the event.
LOCATION:Howell Hall \, 115
SUMMARY:After 9/11: US National Security and Foreign Policy
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/after-911-us-national-security
 -and-foreign-policy
END:VEVENT
