BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar 1 Ics": "https://calendar.unc.edu/event/iah-fellows-forum-its-all-love-beyonce-and-jay-z-and-the-re-enchantment-of-black-romance-in-the-age-of-the-manosphere"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_1_ics
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261010T052519Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53851119526123
DTSTART:20261030T161000Z
DTEND:20261030T170000Z
DESCRIPTION:On Oct. 30\, American studies Assistant Professor Antonia Rando
 lph (FFP ’22) will give a Fellows Forum presentation titled “It’s Al
 l Love? Beyoncé and Jay-Z and the Re-enchantment of Black Romance in the 
 Age of the Manosphere.”\n\n \n\nThis talk will examine Jay-Z and Beyonc
 é’s post-2010 output\, with an emphasis on Everything Is Love (The Cart
 ers\, 2018) and Renaissance (Beyoncé\, 2022)\, to show how their defense 
 of romantic love converts their need to salvage their brand as hip-hop and
  R&B’s happily married power couple into a story about saving the Black 
 nation through heteronormative marriage.\n\n \n\nTo satisfy R&B audiences\
 , they infuse their marriage with Black nationalist significance\, with it
 s survival serving as a proxy for the survival of the Black nation. They l
 ink social mobility and wealth with heteronormative marriage\, simultaneou
 sly turning marriage into an aspirational luxury item while encouraging pe
 ople to look to marriage\, rather than the state\, to escape poverty.\n\n 
 \n\nAt the same time\, they satisfy hip-hop’s norms by constructing Beyo
 ncé as uniquely deserving of empathy as the ultimate “ride or die” ch
 ick\, denying that other Black women deserve the care and consideration sh
 e receives. These individualized\, anti-redistributive responses to shifti
 ng gender norms and the erosion of the social safety net in the post-Civil
  Rights Era provide a cultural commonsense that supports the Black manosph
 ere.\n\n \n\nAntonia Randolph is the Jonathan M. Hess Career Development T
 erm Assistant Professor at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill
 . Her interests include diversity discourse in education\, Black feminist 
 thought\, non-normative Black masculinity and sexuality\, and hip-hop cult
 ure. Her book\, The Wrong Kind of Different: Challenging the Meaning of Di
 versity in American Classrooms (Teachers College Press\, 2012)\, examined 
 the hierarchies elementary school teachers constructed among students of c
 olor. Her current book project\, That’s My Heart: Queering Intimacy in H
 ip-Hop Culture\, forthcoming from the University of California Press\, exa
 mines portrayals of Black men’s intimate relationships in hip-hop cultur
 e.\n\n \n\nRSVP for the Fellows Forum on Jotform.\n\n \n\nSee all events i
 n the IAH Fellows Forum series.
GEO:35.913374;-79.052866
LOCATION:Hyde Hall\, University Room
SUMMARY:IAH Fellows Forum: Antonia Randolph
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/iah-fellows-forum-its-all-love
 -beyonce-and-jay-z-and-the-re-enchantment-of-black-romance-in-the-age-of-t
 he-manosphere
CATEGORIES:Lectures
END:VEVENT
