BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar 1 Ics": "https://calendar.unc.edu/event/john-and-june-allcott-gallery-taylor-barnes-weight-of-light"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_1_ics
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261010T052519Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54187615209544
DTSTART:20261103T130000Z
DTEND:20261103T220000Z
DESCRIPTION:Reception and gallery talk: October 29\, 2026\, 5:00 pm\n\n \n\
 nWeight of Light explores how powerful emotions can be expressed through s
 implicity. Using bold contrasts between light and dark\, the artist create
 s striking images that convey feeling through form\, space\, and gesture r
 ather than detailed storytelling. The exhibition invites viewers to slow d
 own and experience mood\, tension\, and vulnerability in a direct and deep
 ly human way.\n\n \n\nWeight of Light brings together seven works created 
 by artist taylor barnes during her year-long residency at West Orchid in P
 ine Lake\, Georgia. The work marks a period of experimentation and persona
 l reflection. Drawing from her background in fibers and ceramics\, barnes 
 approaches the canvas as a site of material exploration. Through processes
  of staining\, layering\, and transforming solids into liquids and back ag
 ain\, she creates environments that make space for chance\, absence\, and 
 emergence. The seven works resist Western linear narratives\, instead func
 tioning as maps of origin within an ongoing movement from uncertainty towa
 rds revelation. Moving between darkness and light\, the works consider per
 ception\, ambiguity\, and the experience of reclaiming. Existing somewhere
  between painting and object\, these works function as thresholds. As open
 -ended structures\, Weight of Light positions ascension as a practice of s
 elf-possession and power.\n\n \n\ntaylor barnes is a ceramicist and fiber 
 artist exploring spirituality\, oral history\, and power through material 
 and form. Heavily influenced by bell hooks' object/subject theories\, she 
 works with clay\, fabric\, string\, and charcoal to translate emotion and 
 experience into tangible presence. Black being her only color choice and p
 hilosophy\, she creates work that appears restrained yet carries deep labo
 r\, lineage\, ritual\, and quiet intensity. barnes received her MFA in Fib
 ers from the University of North Texas. She lives and works between Georgi
 a and Austin\, Texas.\n\n \n\nAdmission: Free\n\nGallery Hours: Monday-Fri
 day\, 8 am-5 pm\n\n \n\nA weeknight or daytime permit is now required afte
 r 5:00 pm on weekdays. No permit is required from 5:00 pm Friday through 7
 :30 am Monday. A $1.00 one-night pass is available in selected lots. More 
 information can be found HERE.\n\n \n\nArtist website: https://www.barnesa
 rtistry.com/ \n\n \n\nFor more information\, please contact: Annette Lawre
 nce\, annettel@unc.edu \n\n \n\nImage credit: Courtesy of the artist
GEO:35.912392;-79.054366
LOCATION:Hanes Art Center\, John and June Allcott Gallery
SUMMARY:John and June Allcott Gallery: taylor barnes\, Weight of Light
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/john-and-june-allcott-gallery-
 taylor-barnes-weight-of-light
CATEGORIES:Exhibits
END:VEVENT
