BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar 1 Ics": "https://calendar.unc.edu/event/to-beethoven-rather-than-to-seem-part-iii-gut-instinct-chamber-music-project"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_1_ics
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261010T052519Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53825435677972
DTSTART:20270110T210000Z
DTEND:20270110T220000Z
DESCRIPTION:FEATURING\n\nNicholas DiEguenio\, violin\nMimi Solomon\, piano\
 n\n \n\nPROGRAM\n\nFeaturing Beethoven’s heroic and youthful Violin Sona
 ta No. 3 and the Jesse Jones Scherzo it inspired\n\nThis season\, Gut Inst
 inct takes on the large-scale project “To Beethoven Rather Than To Seem\
 ,” marking 200 years since Beethoven’s death and 250 since the U.S. De
 claration of Independence. Over the course of five concerts\, Nicholas DiE
 ugenio and pianist Mimi Solomon will present all ten Beethoven Sonatas on 
 period instruments along with five commissioned works written expressly fo
 r them.\n\n \n\nPARKING\n\nPlease plan to arrive early as parking on campu
 s can be tricky. Learn more about parking and directions to UNC-Chapel Hil
 l’s campus.\n\n \n\nThe Gut Instinct Chamber Music Project\, now in its 
 third season\, is a concert series in the Triangle area which brings UNC-C
 hapel Hill and Duke University’s historical instrument collections toget
 her with artists committed to presenting inspired\, visceral\, historicall
 y informed performances of the 19th-century chamber music repertoire. The 
 series features music of female composers and their artistic circles. The 
 name “Gut Instinct” is a play on words. In 19th-century Europe\, the v
 iolin\, viola\, and cello were strung with (sheep) gut.\n\n \n\nAll of the
  artists collaborating with the Pleyel and Graf pianos at UNC-Chapel Hill 
 (built in 19th-century Paris and Vienna\, respectively)\, as well as the C
 lementi piano at Duke (early 19th-century England) use these gut string ma
 terials. The turn of phrase “gut instinct” also refers to the visceral
 \, accessible nature of the musical performances that this series presents
 . Our mission is to make “Historically Informed Performance” accessibl
 e\, relatable\, and culturally relevant! We also want to feature these spe
 cial pianos that are rarely heard by the public\, along with great music b
 y female composers which has remained lesser known to the greater public f
 or far too long\, and living composers who are keeping our art form alive!
GEO:35.912507;-79.052471
LOCATION:Person Hall
SUMMARY:“To BEethoven Rather Than To Seem”: Part III | Gut Instinct Cha
 mber Music Project
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/to-beethoven-rather-than-to-se
 em-part-iii-gut-instinct-chamber-music-project
CATEGORIES:Performances
END:VEVENT
